      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 118,
      "outputTokens": 973,
      "costUsd": 0.008816,
      "pronunciationText": "Now the hook. At two and a half seconds, v is minus nine quarters, and the acceleration is minus three. The velocity is already negative, and a negative acceleration pushes it further below zero, so the speed grows, from two and a quarter centimetres per second towards three. The robot is speeding up, even though its acceleration is negative. Compare one second. There, v is nine, ay is minus twelve, and the robot is slowing down as it heads for rest. So when velocity and acceleration have the same sign, it speeds up. Opposite signs, it slows down."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-05",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i14/scene-05.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 987,
      "worstWordGap": {
