      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 134,
      "outputTokens": 1051,
      "costUsd": 0.009526,
      "pronunciationText": "We can also go the other way. Here's a different particle. Its acceleration is six t minus four, and it starts at rest at the origin. Find where it is at two seconds. Integrate the acceleration to get velocity: three t squared minus four t, plus a constant. Integration only knows the shape, not where it starts, so we need the starting value. At rest at t equals zero means v is zero there, so the constant is zero. Integrate again: x is t cubed minus two t squared, plus another constant. Starting at the origin makes that zero too. Two steps up need two constants, so we need two starting values."
    },
    {
