      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 161,
      "outputTokens": 1118,
      "costUsd": 0.0101425,
      "pronunciationText": "The same calculus works for any quantity that changes with time. At a ski field, the snow is one hundred centimetres deep, and it's building up at twenty minus two t centimetres per day. So d D by d t is twenty minus two t. Integrate, and use the start. D is one hundred, plus twenty t, minus t squared. The second derivative is minus two, so the build-up rate drops by two centimetres per day, every day. At day five, for example, d D by d t is ten, so the snow is deepening at ten centimetres per day, but that rise is slowing down. The snow is deepest when the rate is zero, at day ten, with two hundred centimetres. After that, the rate is negative, and the snow melts."
    },
    {
